"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/wat-khuha/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/places/wat-khuha/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Temples & Pagodas</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Temples and pagodas</span><h1>Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)</h1><p class="english">Wat Khuha Phimuk</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified 4 items</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="wat-khuha">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/wat-khuha.webp" alt="Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.831526975, 98.367913006 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.831526975,98.367913006" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>A small ordination hall on Khuan Tham in the mining era of Takua Pa</h3><p class="era-years">1970 (2513 BE)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The clearest evidence specifying a construction year at this time is for the temple's small ordination hall, built in 1970 (2513 BE) by Mrs. Huatbi. As for the year the entire temple complex began construction and the reason for choosing the site, there are no confirming documents yet. Therefore, one should not recount history beyond the available evidence.</p><p>This temple emerged during the late era when Takua Pa still had shared memories with tin mining. The town has roots as a port community with trade exchanges among people of many nationalities. Later, the tin mining economy and the Chinese community played a significant role. However, no evidence has yet been found that the builders of the ordination hall or Wat Khuan Tham were directly connected to the governor, the mines, or the Hokkien Chinese group.</p><p>What can be seen of the site's role is its function as a community religious site, with the ordination hall serving as a center for rituals and local art. Inside is a bas-relief stone Buddha image, behind it is a character inscription, and the boundary markers (sema) around the ordination hall are shaped as Buddha images in various postures.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Samnakngan Noyobai lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Conserved Cultural Environment Sites</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · History of Takua Pa</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>From a religious site to a deserted temple list: A transition with gaps</h3><p class="era-years">After 1970 (B.E. 2513) to before 2026 (B.E. 2569) (no year found for individual events)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Official data from the Samnakngan Nayobai Lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat Lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) classifies Wat Khuha Phimuk (Wat Khuan Tham) in the 'temple, abandoned temple, religious site' category. Meanwhile, the central religious property register of the Samnakngan Phraphutthasatsana Haeng Chat (National Office of Buddhism) lists 'Wat Khuan Tham (abandoned)' in Takua Pa district. Thus, it can only be confirmed that the temple is recorded as abandoned in government databases; it is unknown when it became abandoned or for what reason.</p><p>Takua Pa town itself transitioned from a port and mining town. As tin mining operations gradually ceased, economic liveliness decreased, and the old town shifted to being valued as a community heritage. This context helps explain the backdrop of the town's transition, but there is still no direct evidence linking Wat Khuan Tham to the impact of the mine closures.</p><p>No evidence has been found verifying any specific year of restoration, change of ownership, other uses, the 2004 (B.E. 2547) tsunami event, or a specific historical site registration announcement by Krom Sinlapakon (Fine Arts Department) for this temple.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/2021111871cc998239d7c8b8d77aa76b771a377b151224.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาแห่งชาติ (National Office of Buddhism) · National central ecclesiastical property and deserted temples registration data</a> · <a href="https://elibrary.tsri.or.th/fullP/RDG60T0046/RDG60T0046V04/RDG60T0046V04_full.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sthaban Phiphitthaphan Kanrianru Haeng Chat (National Discovery Museum Institute) / Takua Pa Old Town Information Source</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · History of Takua Pa</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>Present day: An art site with a tiny ordination hall, but its operational status is unclear</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>In 2026 (B.E. 2569), discovered official sources still record this place as an "abandoned temple" and do not provide opening hours, admission fees, caretakers, or contact channels. Therefore, it cannot be verified whether the temple is open to visitors or if monks reside there.</p><p>A verified highlight is the very small ordination hall, which ONEP identifies as the smallest in Thailand. Inside, there is a low-relief carved stone Buddha image with an inscription on the back, and an ancient hermit statue in the front.</p><p>No news or public announcements have been found verifying restoration projects, developments, traditional activities, or community use of the area over the past 5 years. Therefore, the current condition of the building and regular activities should be checked with locals or area caretakers before being published as travel information.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Samnakngan Noyobai lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Conserved Cultural Environment Sites</a> · <a href="https://onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/2021111871cc998239d7c8b8d77aa76b771a377b151224.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาแห่งชาติ (National Office of Buddhism) · National central ecclesiastical property and deserted temples registration data</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1970 (B.E. 2513)</p><h3>Construction of the Small Ordination Hall</h3><p>The ordination hall was built by Mrs. Huatbi, featuring a carved stone Buddha image, an inscription, and boundary stones (sema) shaped like Buddha images.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Temple Recorded as Abandoned</h3><p>The ONEP database and the National Office of Buddhism's registry list Wat Khuan Tham as an abandoned temple, though the year it was abandoned is not verified.</p><a href="https://onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/2021111871cc998239d7c8b8d77aa76b771a377b151224.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>No Evidence of Historical Site Registration Found</h3><p>This search did not find a Fine Arts Department registration announcement specifically confirming Wat Khuha Phimuk in Takua Pa.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Small ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The ONEP database identifies it as the smallest ordination hall in Thailand.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Carved stone Buddha</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Inside the ordination hall is a low-relief carved stone Buddha.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Inscription behind the ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>There are inscribed characters behind the ordination hall.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Buddha-shaped boundary markers</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The boundary markers around the hall are shaped like Buddha images in various postures.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Ancient hermit statue</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>An ancient hermit statue stands at the front of the temple.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>ONEP notes that the ordination hall of Wat Khuha Phimuk is the smallest in Thailand.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>There is an inscription behind the ordination hall, and the boundary markers around it are shaped like Buddha images in various postures.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li><a href="/en/places/wat-boromthat/">Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet (the town's relic temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-pathum/">Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-kongkha/">Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-nikorn/">Wat Nikornwararam</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-sena/">Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>The temple's ordination hall was built in 1970 (B.E. 2513) by Mrs. Huat Bi. The ONEP database categorises this temple as a temple, an abandoned temple, and a religious site, stating that the ordination hall is the smallest in Thailand.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>The ONEP database states that it is the smallest ordination hall in Thailand. Inside the ordination hall, there is a low-relief carved stone Buddha image. Behind the ordination hall, there are inscribed characters.</p></details><details><summary>How the municipality document mentions this place</summary><p>Documents mention the small ordination hall and its architectural characteristics.</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) (ONEP) · Conserved Cultural Art Sites</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาแห่งชาติ (National Office of Buddhism) · National central ecclesiastical property and deserted temples registration data</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/2021111871cc998239d7c8b8d77aa76b771a377b151224.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Samnakngan Noyobai lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Conserved Cultural Environment Sites</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · History of Takua Pa</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Old Town Community Research Report</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://elibrary.tsri.or.th/fullP/RDG60T0046/RDG60T0046V04/RDG60T0046V04_full.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>House numbers, streets, and postal codes are not verified from accessible sources.</li><li>Opening hours, admission fees, and recommended visit durations are not verified.</li><li>Phone numbers, Facebook pages, websites, or LINE accounts of the temple are not verified.</li><li>Verified travel methods and distances from Takua Pa town, Phuket, Khao Lak, or the airport are not verified.</li><li>Parking, restrooms, and accessibility for the elderly/wheelchairs are not verified.</li><li>Festivals or traditions linked to the temple from accessible sources are not verified.</li><li>Nearby places confirmed to be within walking distance are not verified.</li><li>Official image links for the location are not verified.</li><li>Official or academic sources confirming its role in the tin mining era, Sino-Portuguese/Chinese architecture, or historical site registration are not verified.</li><li>The founding year, founder, reasons for construction, and specific roles during the era of town rulers/tin mines/maritime trade are not verified.</li><li>The year it became deserted, the cause of abandonment, ownership changes, or other uses are not verified.</li><li>Specific evidence for the temple regarding the impact of the 2004 tsunami is not verified.</li><li>Years and details of renovations are not verified.</li><li>Announcements confirming historical site registration by the Fine Arts Department are not verified.</li><li>Current caretakers, opening hours, admission fees, community activities, and development projects in 2026 are not verified.</li><li>News from the past 5 years directly related to this temple is not verified.</li><li>URLs of old or current photos confirmed to be Wat Khuha Phimuk in Takua Pa are not verified. Do not mix photos of Wat Khuha Phimuk in Yala or Wat Kongkha Phimuk in Takua Pa.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
