favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-kongkha/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-kongkha/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-kongkha/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/wat-kongkha/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/places/wat-kongkha/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Temples & Pagodas</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Temples and pagodas</span><h1>Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</h1><p class="english">Wat Kongkha Phimuk</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">6 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="wat-kongkha">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/wat-kongkha.webp" alt="Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.840837, 98.362899 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.840837,98.362899" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd>OpenStreetMap data via Mapcarta indicates a wheelchair=yes tag, but details on ramp conditions or facilities from official sources are not verified. <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd>076-421-479 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a><a href="https://mapcarta.com/29028704" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Contact information ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>A riverside temple of Takua Pa, starting under the name 'Wat Kongkha'</h3><p class="era-years">1762 (B.E. 2305) (proposed by local and government sources); 1936 (B.E. 2479) (ordination hall)</p><span class="fact-status disputed">Conflicting information</span><p>Evidence from the environmental art agency states the temple was built in 1762 (B.E. 2305) and originally named Wat “Kongkha”, while the archaeological database of Sun Manutsayawitthaya Sirindhorn (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) indicates the age of the important historical site based on the ordination hall built in 1936 (B.E. 2479). The origin should therefore be read as a two-layer history: the temple's age according to registration or accounts, and the age of the surviving buildings, which are not the same.</p><p>The temple is located on an alluvial plain along Khlong Takua Pa, on the west side of the waterway, in a town with a history as a coastal city and a centre for tin mining and maritime trade. This urban context suggests the temple likely served as a religious site for the riverside community. However, no specific evidence for the temple has been found identifying the founder, the reason for construction, or the direct role of Hokkien Chinese or the town governor. Therefore, details beyond the evidence should not be added.</p><p>The ordination hall, built in 1936 (B.E. 2479), is a southern-style brick-and-mortar building facing east, decorated with stucco depicting pagodas, Buddha images, Dharma wheels, and flora. This reflects its role as both a space for religious ceremonies and a showcase of community craftsmanship from the late Rattanakosin period to the reign of King Rama VIII.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4417" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cultural Art Environment (ONEP)</a> · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) Environmental Operation Plan</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>A new name, a new historical site, and returning as a living temple</h3><p class="era-years">1940 (B.E. 2483); 1996 (B.E. 2539); 2002 (B.E. 2545)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The temple history recorded in the archaeological database states that it was originally called Wat Kongkha before being changed to Wat Kongkha Phimuk in 1940 (B.E. 2483). Meanwhile, the ordination hall and pagoda are still in use and have not become ruins isolated from the temple. The different names in documents should be preserved to show readers the site's development.</p><p>Krom Sinlapakon (Fine Arts Department) announced the registration of the ordination hall and pagoda, along with designating the historical site boundaries, in the Royal Gazette on 18 December 1996 (B.E. 2539). The registration ensures the heritage value of the old buildings is protected as an artistic legacy. Concurrently, it remains a private temple affiliated with the Maha Nikaya sect, with resident monks.</p><p>The provincial dharma practice centre registry of Samnakngan Phraphutthasatsana Haeng Chat (National Office of Buddhism) lists “Kongkha Phimuk” as the 1st dharma practice centre of Phang Nga province according to a 2002 (B.E. 2545) resolution. Currently, the archaeological database indicates that it is maintained and generally in stable condition. However, no evidence has been found verifying if the area was affected by the 2004 (B.E. 2547) tsunami or if it had a specific period of abandonment or restoration.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</a> · <a href="https://www.onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/20230608fc490ca45c00b1249bbe3554a4fdf6fb104517.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาแห่งชาติ (National Office of Buddhism) Provincial Meditation Center Registry</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>A historical site that still breathes: An active temple and meditation center</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>In the latest published information, Wat Kongkha Phimuk still has resident monks and operates as a private temple affiliated with the Maha Nikaya sect. The maintaining agency is stated to be the temple itself, with the archaeological database advising to contact Phra Khru Nanthapanyakon, the abbot, at 076-421-479.</p><p>The ordination hall and pagoda are active historical sites. The temple interior is described as clean, shady, and suitable for religious practices. The temple also serves as the 1st provincial dharma practice centre of Phang Nga, making it a place where people can pay respects to Buddha, admire the stucco work, and engage in dharma practice.</p><p>Activities with cited sources include dharma practice in honour of the monarchy, overnight New Year prayers, Brahmin nun ordination for royal merit, and summer novice ordination. However, no 2026 (B.E. 2569) announcements have been found verifying activity schedules, opening hours, new restoration projects, or facility details. Therefore, you should call the temple before travelling.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</a> · <a href="https://www.onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/20230608fc490ca45c00b1249bbe3554a4fdf6fb104517.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาแห่งชาติ (National Office of Buddhism) Provincial Meditation Center Registry</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status disputed">Conflicting information</span><p class="era-years">1762 (B.E. 2305)</p><h3>Founding of Wat Kongkha (Data conflicts with building age evidence)</h3><p>ONEP states the temple was built in 1762 and originally named Wat Kongkha, but the archaeological database uses 1936 as the age of the ubosot, so it cannot be concluded whether the site and the building started in the same year.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4417" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1936 (B.E. 2479)</p><h3>Construction of the ubosot which is an important historical site</h3><p>The rectangular brick and mortar ubosot faces east and features stucco designs and southern Thai style roof structures.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status disputed">Conflicting information</span><p class="era-years">1940 (B.E. 2483)</p><h3>Renamed to Wat Kongkha Phimuk</h3><p>The temple history in the archaeological database states that it was originally named Wat Kongkha and then renamed to Wat Kongkha Phimuk.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">18 December 1996 (2539)</p><h3>Registered as a historical site</h3><p>The Fine Arts Department announced the registration of the historical site and defined the land area in the Royal Gazette Volume 113, Special Part 50 Ngor.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">2002 (B.E. 2545)</p><h3>Became the first provincial meditation practice center of Phang Nga</h3><p>The National Office of Buddhism registry lists Wat Kongkha Phimuk as the first center of Phang Nga province according to resolution 12/2002.</p><a href="https://www.onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/20230608fc490ca45c00b1249bbe3554a4fdf6fb104517.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Historical site ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>View the brick and stucco building. The gable and door or window arches are decorated with stucco art, including Buddha images and floral patterns.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Pagoda</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>It is one of the important historical sites within the temple and remains part of the active temple.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Atmosphere along the Takua Pa Canal</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The temple is located on the western bank of the Takua Pa Canal, with the canal situated to the east of the temple.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Stucco details on the gable</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The front features a pagoda, Buddha images in the gazing and alms-bowl holding postures, while the back features the Dharmachakra and a Naga-protected Buddha.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The name 'Kongkha Phimuk' appears as the first entry in the Phang Nga provincial meditation practice center register.</p><a href="https://www.onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/20230608fc490ca45c00b1249bbe3554a4fdf6fb104517.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The ordination hall and pagoda still serve as active historical sites and are not closed off merely as archaeological viewing spots.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>From the Takua Pa Town Municipality office, head west along Phetkasem Road / Highway 4 for about 5 kilometers, turn left onto Rat Bamrung Road, go straight for about 280 meters, then turn left along the path. The temple will be on the right.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Airport</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li>Somchai Andaman Building <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/29028704" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li>Kanda Supermarket <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/29028704" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-boromthat/">Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet (the town's relic temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-khuha/">Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-pathum/">Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-nikorn/">Wat Nikornwararam</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-sena/">Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>Getting there from Takua Pa Town</summary><p>From the Takua Pa Town Municipality office, head west along Phetkasem Road / Highway 4 for about 5 kilometers, turn left onto Rat Bamrung Road, go straight for about 280 meters, then turn left along the path. The temple will be on the right.</p></details><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>The ordination hall and pagoda are active historical sites, and the temple serves as the first provincial meditation practice center of Phang Nga. The Fine Arts Department (Krom Sinlapakon) announced its registration as a historical site and defined its land boundaries in the Royal Gazette on December 18, 1996 (2539 BE).</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>Observe the brick and mortar building, with its pediment and arched doors and windows decorated with stucco, including Buddha images and floral patterns. It is one of the important historical sites within the temple and is still in an active temple. The temple is located on the west bank of Khlong Takua Pa, with the canal situated to the east of the temple.</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Cultural Art Environment (ONEP)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4417" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mapcarta (Data from OpenStreetMap)</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://mapcarta.com/29028704" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">TAT Media Assets</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://tatmediaassets.com/file/1097442" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Thailand YellowPages</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.yellowpages.co.th/profile/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A0%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%82-nabHw5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Samnakngan Phraphutthasatsana Haeng Chat (National Office of Buddhism)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/20230608fc490ca45c00b1249bbe3554a4fdf6fb104517.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) Environmental Operation Plan</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>Published and verified opening hours are not verified.</li><li>Admission fee data is not verified.</li><li>Facebook pages, websites, or LINE accounts confirmed to belong to the temple are not verified.</li><li>A postal code verified to match the temple from primary sources is not verified.</li><li>Routes and distances that can be verified from Phuket, Khao Lak, or the airport are not verified.</li><li>Data confirming the temple's role in the tin mining era is not verified.</li><li>Evidence confirming Sino-Portuguese or Chinese architecture is not verified.</li><li>Data confirming parking, restrooms, and recommended visit times are not verified.</li><li>Months or lunar calendar dates for the festivals are not verified.</li><li>Walking times to nearby places are not verified.</li><li>Evidence identifying the founder and reason for construction in 1762 is not verified.</li><li>Sources provide different years for the temple's start and the ordination hall's construction, so the temple's age cannot be summarized into a single year.</li><li>Evidence specifically for the temple regarding its role in tin mining, maritime trade, Hokkien Chinese, or the town ruler system is not verified.</li><li>Evidence confirming it was abandoned or damaged by the 2004 tsunami is not verified.</li><li>News from the past 5 years confirming renovation or development projects specifically for the temple are not verified.</li><li>Old photographs with verifiable sources are not verified.</li><li>A 2026 announcement regarding opening hours, activity schedules, and facilities is not verified.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
.\audit\jobs\en-attrs.log:879:D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\places\wat-nikorn\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Wat Nikornwararam · Wat Yanyao, Takua Pa · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Retelling the site&#x27;s history through its role in the community · Takuapa temple · Phang Nga temple · old temple in Takuapa"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Wat Nikornwararam · Wat Yanyao, Takua Pa"><meta property="og:description" content="Retelling the site&#x27;s history through its role in the community · Takuapa temple · Phang Nga temple · old temple in Takuapa"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-nikorn/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-nikorn.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-nikorn.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-nikorn/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Wat Nikornwararam (Wat Yan Yao) · Wat Takua Pa", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-nikorn/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "TouristAttraction", "name": "Wat Nikornwararam (Wat Yan Yao)", "url": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-nikorn/", "image": "https://takuapa101.com/assets/wat-nikorn.webp", "description": "Documents link the temple with the community and the 2004 tsunami (พ.ศ. 2547).", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "streetAddress": "Yan Yao village, Thanon Phet Kasem", "addressLocality": "Takua Pa", "addressRegion": "Phang Nga", "addressCountry": "TH"}, "geo": {"@type": "GeoCoordinates", "latitude": 8.8691760396, "longitude": 98.348042969}}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "How should visitors dress?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Dress respectfully when entering a religious site."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the history of this place?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The temple was built in 1793 (พ.ศ. 2336) by Khun Ta Kaew and Khun Ta Ram. It received royal consecration of its ecclesiastical boundary in 1842 (พ.ศ. 2385)."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is there to see?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Confirmed highlights are its founding in 1793 (พ.ศ. 2336) and royal consecration of its ecclesiastical boundary in 1842 (พ.ศ. 2385). The temple was once used to gather victims’ remains while they awaited identity verification."}}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-nikorn/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-nikorn/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-nikorn/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/wat-nikorn/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/places/wat-nikorn/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Temples & Pagodas</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Temples and pagodas</span><h1>Wat Nikornwararam</h1><p class="english">Wat Nikornwararam</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">9 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="wat-nikorn">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/wat-nikorn.webp" alt="Wat Nikornwararam" width="1000" height="726" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.8691760396, 98.348042969 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.8691760396,98.348042969" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4416" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd>Polite dress is recommended when entering the religious site. <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>A community temple in early Takua Pa</h3><p class="era-years">1793 to 1842 (พ.ศ. 2336 to 2385)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Wat Nikon Wararam, or Wat Yan Yao, was initially built in 1793 (2336 BE) by Khun Ta Kaew and Khun Ta Ram. The discovered evidence verifies the year and the names of the founders, but does not provide detailed reasons on what specific mission the temple was established for. Therefore, it should be viewed as a religious site of the local community forming in the Takua Pa basin, rather than tying it to stories without direct evidence.</p><p>The broader picture of Takua Pa town has a history as a port town and tin mining source, with maritime trade and various groups of people. However, no temple-specific evidence has been found to verify the role of Wat Nikon Wararam regarding tin mines, maritime trade, Hokkien Chinese people, or the governor system. Thus, no conclusions are made beyond the data sources.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4416" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Samnakngan Noyobai lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Conserved Cultural Environment Sites</a> · <a href="https://anyflip.com/vlkdm/ecrk/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Takola in the past... Takua Pa in the present</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>From an ancient temple to a tsunami memorial and land management</h3><p class="era-years">1842 to 2022 (พ.ศ. 2385 to 2565)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The temple received the royal granting of the land for a temple in 1842 (2385 BE), which is a crucial piece of evidence that the religious site was certified as an area for monastic activities according to the Sangha's tradition at the time. However, no documents have been found specifying major restorations, changes in ownership, or periods of abandonment.</p><p>After the tsunami on December 26, 2004 (2547 BE), the temple was used as a gathering place for the bodies of disaster victims awaiting forensic identification. This temporary role made Wat Yan Yao a part of Takua Pa's disaster response history. Later, on October 30, 2022 (2565 BE), the Samnakngan Phraphutthasatsana Changwat Phangnga (Phang Nga Provincial Office of Buddhism) participated in handing over the land title deed document to the abbot, serving as evidence of land ownership management in the modern era.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4416" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning)</a> · <a href="https://pna.onab.go.th/th/content/page/index/id/34431" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Samnakngan Phraphutthasatsana Changwat Phangnga (Phang Nga Provincial Office of Buddhism) · Handing over land title deed document</a> · <a href="https://www.cifs.go.th/Files/news/file/ff82ea76-112f-4392-a8ff-e485e9fbc04e.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sathaban Niti Witthayasat (Central Institute of Forensic Science) · Event record on December 28, 2004 (2547 BE)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>A temple still serving as a centre for faith and public works</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The latest discovered evidence verifies that the temple is still in use, with Phra Khru Nikon Thammasophit as the abbot, and has monks in residence during the Buddhist Lent. The temple database indicates the organization of the united Kathina ceremony in 2024 (2567 BE) and 2025 (2568 BE), while government/local news verifies the temple's participation in district and provincial level activities.</p><p>In recent years, the temple has been both a space for religious activities and a public space: in 2024 (2567 BE) it was used as a donation center to help northern flood victims, and there was an inspection visit for the Temple, People, State, Creating Happiness project as a southern regional model temple. However, no data has been found verifying the opening and closing hours for tourists, detailed building conditions, or temple-specific restoration projects in 2026 (2569 BE).</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://pna.onab.go.th/th/content/page/index/id/34431" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาจังหวัดพังงา (Phang Nga Provincial Office of Buddhism)</a> · <a href="https://www.dmc.tv/wat/13166" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DMC · 10,000 Temples United Kathina Project across Thailand</a> · <a href="https://archive.siamrath.co.th/n/568331" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Siam Rath · Donation center to help flood victims</a> · <a href="https://www.ride-explorer.com/?p=7378" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ride Explorer · Model temple inspection visit</a> · <a href="https://www.topnews.co.th/news/1546452" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TOPNEWS · Preserving Takua Pa old town Songkran festival</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1793 (2336 BE)</p><h3>Beginning of temple construction</h3><p>Khun Ta Kaeo and Khun Ta Ram built Wat Nikornwararam.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4416" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1842 (2385 BE)</p><h3>Received Wisungkhammasima</h3><p>The temple was granted Wisungkhammasima.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4416" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">December 26, 2004 (2547 BE)</p><h3>Post tsunami memorial area</h3><p>Used to gather bodies of victims waiting for identity verification.</p><a href="https://www.cifs.go.th/Files/news/file/ff82ea76-112f-4392-a8ff-e485e9fbc04e.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">October 30, 2022 (2565 BE)</p><h3>Received land title deed documents</h3><p>The Phang Nga Provincial Office of Buddhism and the Land Office handed over the title deed documents to the abbot.</p><a href="https://pna.onab.go.th/th/content/page/index/id/34431" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">September 22, 2024 (2567 BE)</p><h3>Donation center for northern floods</h3><p>The temple, together with the monastic community and the people of Takua Pa, collected items to help flood victims.</p><a href="https://archive.siamrath.co.th/n/568331" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">2025 (2568 BE)</p><h3>Participated in Takua Pa old town Songkran event</h3><p>The abbot and monks of Wat Nikornwararam participated in the alms offering activity organized by the province.</p><a href="https://www.topnews.co.th/news/1546452" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>An ancient temple of the Yan Yao community</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Confirmed highlights include its establishment in 1793 CE (2336 BE) and receiving its royal boundary in 1842 CE.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4416" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>A space of tsunami memory</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The temple was used to gather the bodies of tsunami victims awaiting identification.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4416" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2025-04-13 · TOPNEWS</p><h3>Phang Nga continues Songkran traditions with alms offering in Takua Pa old town</h3><p>Phra Khru Nikorn Thammasophit and monks from Wat Nikornwararam participated in provincial activities.</p><a href="https://www.topnews.co.th/news/1546452" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2025-01-01 · Ride Explorer</p><h3>Inspection of the Temple, Public, State, Creating Happiness project</h3><p>Wat Nikornwararam was mentioned as a temple ready for southern regional assessment.</p><a href="https://www.ride-explorer.com/?p=7378" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2024-09-22 · Siam Rath</p><h3>Phang Nga monks unite to help flood victims in the north</h3><p>The temple set up a donation center for items together with the monastic community and the public.</p><a href="https://archive.siamrath.co.th/n/568331" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2022-10-30 · Samnakngan Phraphutthasatsana Changwat Phangnga (Phang Nga Provincial Office of Buddhism)</p><h3>Phang Nga Provincial Office of Buddhism joins in handing over the land title deed documents of Wat Nikornwararam</h3><p>Handing over the land title deed documents to Phra Khru Nikorn Thammasophit, the abbot of Wat Nikornwararam.</p><a href="https://pna.onab.go.th/th/content/page/index/id/34431" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Wat Yan Yao was used as a gathering place for the bodies of 2004 CE tsunami victims awaiting identification.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4416" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>In 2024 CE, the temple served as a donation centre to help flood victims in the North.</p><a href="https://archive.siamrath.co.th/n/568331" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>No verified route recommendations were found from the available sources.</p></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>No verified route recommendations were found from the available sources.</p></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>No verified route recommendations were found from the available sources.</p></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Airport</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>No verified route recommendations were found from the available sources.</p></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li>Takua Pa Riverside Market, Yan Yao Walking Street <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZeycJnHU_Nw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-boromthat/">Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet (the town's relic temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-khuha/">Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-pathum/">Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-kongkha/">Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-sena/">Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>How to dress</summary><p>Polite dress is recommended when entering the religious site.</p></details><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>The temple was built in 1793 (2336 BE) by Khun Ta Kaew and Khun Ta Ram. The temple received the royal granting of the land for a temple in 1842 (2385 BE).</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>Verified highlights are its founding history in 1793 (2336 BE) and receiving the royal granting of the land for a temple in 1842 (2385 BE). The temple was used to gather bodies of disaster victims awaiting forensic identification.</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) (ONEP) · Conserved Cultural Art Sites</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4416" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Wat Nikon Wararam · ClassStart Books</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://books.classstart.org/thai-buddhist-temples/03/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Strolling and eating at Takua Pa riverside market, Yan Yao walking street · YouTube</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZeycJnHU_Nw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Samnakngan Noyobai lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Conserved Cultural Environment Sites</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4416" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาจังหวัดพังงา (Phang Nga Provincial Office of Buddhism)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://pna.onab.go.th/th/content/page/index/id/34431" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สถาบันนิติวิทยาศาสตร์ (Central Institute of Forensic Science) · Tsunami incident record</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.cifs.go.th/Files/news/file/ff82ea76-112f-4392-a8ff-e485e9fbc04e.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">DMC · Wat Nikornwararam</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.dmc.tv/wat/13166" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สยามรัฐ (Siam Rath) · Flood-relief donation center</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://archive.siamrath.co.th/n/568331" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">TOPNEWS · Songkran in Takua Pa Old Town</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.topnews.co.th/news/1546452" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Ride Explorer · Southern regional model temple</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.ride-explorer.com/?p=7378" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Takola in the past... Takua Pa in the present</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://anyflip.com/vlkdm/ecrk/basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>Verified opening hours could not be found from the read sources.</li><li>Verified admission fees, phone numbers, Facebook pages, websites, or LINE accounts could not be found.</li><li>A verified postcode could not be found, and secondary sources list a different subdistrict from official records.</li><li>No evidence confirms its role in the tin-mining era, Sino-Portuguese/Chinese architecture, or registration as a historical site.</li><li>Verified information on parking, restrooms, wheelchair access, and temple festivals could not be found.</li><li>Official image links with specified usage rights are not verified.</li><li>No temple-specific evidence confirms it was established for tin-mining activities, maritime trade, Hokkien Chinese, or the town governor system.</li><li>The year and details of restoration, abandonment, or historical site registration remain undiscovered.</li><li>Verified data on building conditions, restoration plans, opening hours, and annual activities in 2026 CE have not been found.</li><li>Due to conflicting subdistrict records in some sources, the official coordinates and lists from ONEP and the Phang Nga Provincial Office of Buddhism are used.</li><li>Some sources give conflicting subdistrict names, so the coordinates and official listings from สผ./สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาจังหวัดพังงา are treated as primary.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
.\audit\jobs\en-attrs.log:880:D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\places\wat-pathum\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Wat Pathum Thara · Wat Na Mueang Takua Pa · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="From an old temple to tales of the waterway · Wat Pathum Thara Phang Nga · Takua Pa old town temple"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Wat Pathum Thara · Wat Na Mueang Takua Pa"><meta property="og:description" content="From an old temple to tales of the waterway · Wat Pathum Thara Phang Nga · Takua Pa old town temple"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-pathum/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-pathum.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-pathum.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-pathum/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Wat Pathum Thara (Wat Na Mueang), Takua Pa", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-pathum/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "TouristAttraction", "name": "Wat Pathum Thara (Wat Na Mueang)", "url": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-pathum/", "image": "https://takuapa101.com/assets/wat-pathum.webp", "description": "An old ordination hall and the story of water transport link this place to the town’s trading history.", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "streetAddress": "Thanon Na Mueang, Talat Yai community", "addressLocality": "Takua Pa", "addressRegion": "Phang Nga", "postalCode": "82110", "addressCountry": "TH"}, "geo": {"@type": "GeoCoordinates", "latitude": 8.8282064, "longitude": 98.3667554}}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "What are the opening hours?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Information not verified · Love Thailand lists 08:00 to 17:00 daily, but no announcement from the temple has confirmed it."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the history of this place?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Municipal documents describe the ordination hall as Southern vernacular art with wooden columns as its structural core. The municipality states that the Fine Arts Department registered it as an ancient monument in 1935 (พ.ศ. 2478); this research has not checked the original registration announcement."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is there to see?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The structural details mentioned in municipal documents suit close observation of local craftsmanship; there is no information confirming whether all of them can be seen from outside. Boat parts that sources say are kept in the temple grounds link the site to the town’s waterborne goods transport. No latest condition check is available for 2026 (พ.ศ. 2569)."}}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-pathum/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-pathum/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-pathum/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/wat-pathum/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/places/wat-pathum/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Temples & Pagodas</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Temples and pagodas</span><h1>Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</h1><p class="english">Wat Pathum Thara</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">8 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="wat-pathum">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/wat-pathum.webp" alt="Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.8282064, 98.3667554 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.8282064,98.3667554" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/815944559" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd>Love Thailand website specifies every day 08:00 to 17:00, but there is no verified announcement from the temple. <span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://www.lovethailand.org/travel/en/68-Phang-Nga/7958-Wat-Na-Mueang.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>Wat Na Mueang and the restoration of Takua Pa</h3><p class="era-years">1777 (2320 BE) or during the town's revival in the reign of King Rama III (still debated)</p><span class="fact-status disputed">Conflicting information</span><p>The origins of Wat Pathum Thara or Wat Na Mueang are told in two ways. Tesaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality) records the oral history that Lady Thao, daughter of Phraya Takua Pa (Muang), built the temple in 1777 (2320 BE). However, the ONEP old town database explains that after the old town was destroyed and abandoned during the Burmese war, the town's revival at Ban Talat Yai in the reign of King Rama III started with the construction of Wat Na Mueang, followed by the governor's residence nearby.</p><p>The big picture of Takua Pa is a riverine port town and a center for the tin trade. The town prospered from mining and maritime trade. Chinese laborers, especially Hokkien Chinese, settled and connected the culture with the Malay Peninsula. The temple likely served as the main religious site of the Talat Yai community and a landmark for the newly establishing town, but found sources do not identify a confirmed builder or its daily ritual roles in that era.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning): Takua Pa Old Town</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Talat Yai</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>From a water port to an old town district</h3><p class="era-years">1867 to 1956, and 1935 (พ.ศ. 2410 to 2499, and พ.ศ. 2478)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>During the era of water trade, the temple was near areas with traffic and goods transport. Community sources indicate that a boiler in the temple grounds is a part of a steamboat that Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch na Nakhon) co-invested to buy in 1867 (2410 BE) to transport goods and suppress pirates, before the boat deteriorated and sank in the Takua Pa River. This evidence reflects trade networks but is not evidence that the temple operated mining businesses itself.</p><p>When the town center moved to Ban Yan Yao in 1913 (2456 BE) and waterways became shallow, Ban Talat Yai's economic role declined. The temple remained a religious site and community space. The temple's monks' living quarters were initially used as classrooms for Rongrian Tesaban Ban Si Takua Pa (Ban Si Takua Pa Municipal School) before the school moved to its current location in 1956 (2499 BE). The municipality notes that the southern vernacular ordination hall was registered as an ancient monument in 1935 (2478 BE), but the original announcement has not been checked in this research phase.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Talat Yai</a> · <a href="https://www.muangboranjournal.com/post/English-school-Takuapa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">วารสารเมืองโบราณ (Muang Boran Journal): The first English school in Takua Pa</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>A religious site in the Talat Yai community whose status requires on-site checking</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p>The verified information confirms the temple's location on Thanon Na Mueang in the Talat Yai community and confirms the value of the old ordination hall as a heritage of the old town. However, no official announcements or news in the last 5 years have been found that confirm restoration projects, regular activities, or direct visiting conditions of the temple.</p><p>A travel website lists opening hours as 08:00 to 17:00 daily, but no announcement from the temple or a government agency has confirmed this. Treat it as unverified information and contact the site before travelling. Access to the inside of the ordination hall, the condition of the steam boiler, care by the monastic community, and community activities in 2026 (พ.ศ. 2569) still require field research.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</a> · <a href="https://www.lovethailand.org/travel/th/68-%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%B2/7958-%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B7%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%87.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Love Thailand: Wat Na Mueang</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status disputed">Conflicting information</span><p class="era-years">1777 (2320 BE)</p><h3>Tale of the Temple's Construction</h3><p>The municipality records a tale that Than Thao, daughter of Phraya Takua Pa (Muang), built Wat Na Mueang this year, but another set of evidence links the temple to the town's revival during King Rama III's reign.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1867 (2410 BE)</p><h3>Steamboat and the Takua Pa River</h3><p>The steam boiler in the temple area is linked to a steamboat used to transport goods and suppress pirates, co-purchased by Phraya Senanuchit and Phraya Wichit Songkhram.</p><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1935 (2478 BE)</p><h3>Ordination Hall Registered as a Historical Site</h3><p>The municipality notes that the Fine Arts Department registered the ordination hall, but the original announcement is not verified.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1956 (2499 BE)</p><h3>Monk Cells as Initial Classrooms</h3><p>The Ban Si Takua Pa Municipal School once held classes in the temple's monk cells before moving to its current location.</p><a href="https://www.muangboranjournal.com/post/English-school-Takuapa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Town Revival at Ban Talat Yai</h3><p>The ONEP database states that the construction of the new town began with building Wat Na Mueang before constructing the governor's residence nearby.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Old ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>See the religious building that the municipality identifies as southern art and registered as a historical site. It is not verified if the interior is open on visit days.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Wooden core pillars of the ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Structural details mentioned in municipal documents, suitable for observing local craftsmanship. It is not verified whether they are fully visible from the outside.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Steamboat boiler</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>A boat part that sources say is kept within the temple grounds, serving as evidence linked to the town's water transport. Its latest condition in 2026 is not verified.</p><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The steam boiler in the temple grounds is said to be a part of a steamboat used to transport goods and suppress pirates in 1867.</p><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The temple's monk quarters were once used as an early classroom for the Ban Si Takua Pa Municipal School.</p><a href="https://www.muangboranjournal.com/post/English-school-Takuapa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li><a href="/en/places/wat-boromthat/">Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet (the town's relic temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-khuha/">Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-kongkha/">Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-nikorn/">Wat Nikornwararam</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-sena/">Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>Opening hours</summary><p>Information not verified · Love Thailand lists 08:00 to 17:00 daily, but no announcement from the temple has confirmed it</p></details><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>Municipal documents indicate that the ordination hall is of southern vernacular art and uses wooden pillars as its core. The municipality notes that Krom Sinlapakon (Fine Arts Department) registered the ordination hall as an ancient monument in 1935 (2478 BE); this research has not yet checked the original registration announcement.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>The structural details mentioned in the municipal documents are suitable for observing vernacular craftsmanship; there is no information yet on whether it is fully visible from the outside. The boat part, which sources indicate is located in the temple grounds, is evidence connected to the town's water transport of goods. However, there is no latest condition check information in 2026 (2569 BE).</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Town Municipality (เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า): Environmental Action Plan for Fiscal Year 2020 (พ.ศ. 2563), pages 11 to 12</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Talat Yai</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning): Takua Pa Old Town</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Muang Boran Journal: English School at Takua Pa during the reign of King Rama V</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.muangboranjournal.com/post/English-school-Takuapa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Love Thailand: Wat Na Mueang</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.lovethailand.org/travel/en/68-Phang-Nga/7958-Wat-Na-Mueang.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">วารสารเมืองโบราณ (Muang Boran Journal): The first English school in Takua Pa</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.muangboranjournal.com/post/English-school-Takuapa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Love Thailand: Wat Na Mueang</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.lovethailand.org/travel/th/68-%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%B2/7958-%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B7%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%87.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>GPS coordinates are not verified from Google Maps, OpenStreetMap, Mapcarta, or Wikidata.</li><li>Address: The road name and entrance should be checked on-site. House numbers are not verified.</li><li>The English name uses the name set by the employer. An official English name is not verified.</li><li>Opening hours are only found on travel websites and old data. Current schedules or ordination hall opening times are not verified.</li><li>Admission fees, phone numbers, Facebook pages, websites, and LINE accounts of the temple are not verified.</li><li>Travel methods from Takua Pa town, Phuket, and Khao Lak are not verified.</li><li>Founding year and builder: Primary evidence to decide this is not verified.</li><li>Registration: The original announcement, volume, part, date, and land boundaries are not verified.</li><li>Role in the tin mining era: Evidence that the temple owned mines or was directly an ore port is not verified.</li><li>Chinese or Sino-Portuguese architecture of the temple: Specific evidence for the temple buildings is not verified.</li><li>Recommended visit times, duration, and specific dress codes for the temple are not verified.</li><li>Temple-specific festivals, months, lunar calendar dates, and activities are not verified.</li><li>Parking, restrooms, ramps, and accessibility for the elderly or wheelchairs are not verified.</li><li>Nearby places within walking distance are not verified.</li><li>Official images with verified rights are not verified.</li><li>The current condition or access to the ordination hall, wooden pillar details, and the steam boiler are not verified.</li><li>The verified founding year and builder still conflict between the 1777 account and the story of the town's revival in the reign of King Rama III.</li><li>A Royal Gazette announcement or original Fine Arts Department database confirming the 1935 registration is not verified.</li><li>Evidence confirming direct impacts from the 2004 tsunami is not verified.</li><li>News from the past 5 years specifically matching the temple, or renovation/development projects during that period, are not verified.</li><li>Direct image URLs for the temple with verifiable credits and rights are not verified.</li><li>It is not verified whether the temple currently opens its ordination hall for viewing, what the interior condition is like, and what regular activities exist.</li><li>The name of the temple caretaker or the latest renovation data is not verified.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
.\audit\jobs\en-attrs.log:881:D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\places\wat-sena\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan · Wat Mai Kampang · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Observing the coexistence of Thai and Chinese elements · Wat Mai Kamphaeng · Takuapa old town temple · ancient temple in Takuapa"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan · Wat Mai Kampang"><meta property="og:description" content="Observing the coexistence of Thai and Chinese elements · Wat Mai Kamphaeng · Takuapa old town temple · ancient temple in Takuapa"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-sena/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-sena.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-sena.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-sena/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Wat Senanuch Rangsan (Wat Mai), Takua Pa", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-sena/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "TouristAttraction", "name": "Wat Senanuch Rangsan (Wat Mai)", "url": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-sena/", "image": "https://takuapa101.com/assets/wat-sena.webp", "description": "The ordination hall’s architecture includes Chinese elements and reflects Thai-Chinese culture.", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "streetAddress": "Old Market, Takua Pa Subdistrict", "addressLocality": "Takua Pa", "addressRegion": "Phang Nga", "addressCountry": "TH"}, "geo": {"@type": "GeoCoordinates", "latitude": 8.8302299579, "longitude": 98.3624509671}}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the history of this place?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The temple was founded in 1847 (พ.ศ. 2390) by Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon), ruler of Takua Pa. The temple register gives the founding year as 1847 (พ.ศ. 2390) and identifies it as a Maha Nikaya public temple."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is there to see?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "See the large ordination hall, which the ONEP database says was influenced by Chinese architecture. A tourism source says there is a museum displaying antiques, including a wooden pulpit bearing the initials จปร. Check on-site whether it is open to visitors. See the temple grounds registered as an ancient monument in 1996 (พ.ศ. 2539)."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How do municipal documents describe this place?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The ordination hall’s architecture includes Chinese elements and reflects Thai-Chinese culture."}}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-sena/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-sena/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-sena/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/wat-sena/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/places/wat-sena/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Temples & Pagodas</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Temples and pagodas</span><h1>Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</h1><p class="english">Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">9 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="wat-sena">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/wat-sena.webp" alt="Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.8302299579, 98.3624509671 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.8302299579,98.3624509671" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>Wat Mai Kamphaeng: The governor's temple in the mining town</h3><p class="era-years">Around 1847 (2390 BE)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Wat Senanucharangsan, or locally called “Wat Mai Kamphaeng”, emerged during the period when Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch na Nakhon) was the governor of Takua Pa. Sources from the Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre state that the temple was built after the town walls, government offices, and the governor's residence were completed. Thus, it is significant as both a Buddhist offering and a memorial to its builder.</p><p>The original area was a mound at the foot of Khao Chamonkhiri. Phraya Senanuchit had the area leveled, then built a brick and mortar wall about 2 meters high, with east and west gates, before constructing an ordination hall in the middle of the temple and two preaching halls on the sides. The name “Wat Mai Kamphaeng” (New Walled Temple) was therefore not just a name for a new temple, but reflected the town landscape taking shape along with administrative power and a community center.</p><p>In the era when Takua Pa was still an Andaman port town, connecting maritime trade and the tin economy, the temple's ordination hall was used as the site for the oath of allegiance ceremony. This shows that the temple directly connected religious rituals with the town's political order. Meanwhile, the ordination hall's style, which received Chinese influence, also aligned with the market district and Takua Pa's Hokkien Chinese community, but sources do not confirm that the craftsmen or patrons were directly Chinese.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · Encyclopedia of Thai Culture</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning (ONEP)</a> · <a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">วารสารปาริชาต มหาวิทยาลัยทักษิณ (Parichart Journal, Thaksin University)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>From a ceremonial temple to old town heritage: Repairs, changes, and registration</h3><p class="era-years">1865 to 2023 (พ.ศ. 2408 to 2566)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The temple continued to host the oath-taking water ceremony before the 1932 change in government (พ.ศ. 2475). Temple records also note that Phra Khru Udom Panya (Nuam) served as abbot from 1865 to 1909 (พ.ศ. 2408 to 2452), and that his disciples later built a stupa to hold his ashes around 2014 (พ.ศ. 2557). This gives the temple layers of memory belonging both to the town and to the local monastic community.</p><p>The buildings do not entirely retain their original forms. The Encyclopedia of Thai Culture states that the ordination hall once had a three-tiered roof covered with scaled tiles, but it was repaired by reducing it to a single tier and replacing it with corrugated iron. Another set of temple registry data indicates that the current preaching hall was built in 1963 (2506 BE) and lists the date of receiving royal boundary consecration (Visungkhamasima) as 8 January 1898 (2440 BE). However, some year details in secondary sources have typing or scanning errors, so they are not used as evidence to confirm additional restoration years.</p><p>Krom Sinlapakon (Fine Arts Department) registered the temple's ancient monument status in 1996 (2539 BE), with the highlights being the wall around the temple and the large ordination hall with Chinese influence. Later, in the context of the Takua Pa old town revival, the temple is also home to the Takua Pa Community Museum, which collects utensils and equipment from the mining way of life. This makes the temple area function as both a religious site and a space recounting the town's memories after the mining era.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · Encyclopedia of Thai Culture</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ONEP · Cultural environments for conservation</a> · <a href="https://books.classstart.org/thai-buddhist-temples/03/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wat Senanucharangsan · Temple Database (ClassStart Books)</a> · <a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">วารสารปาริชาต มหาวิทยาลัยทักษิณ (Parichart Journal, Thaksin University)</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>An active historical site, but access must be checked locally</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>หลักฐานล่าสุดที่เข้าถึงได้ยืนยันว่า วัดเสนานุชรังสรรค์ยังถูกนำเสนอเป็นแหล่งเรียนรู้และแหล่งท่องเที่ยวของเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่ามีสื่อแนะนำวัด และในปี 2566 เผยแพร่กิจกรรมแห่เทียน · เวียนเมืองพร้อมพิธีบวงสรวงเพื่อขออนุญาตบูรณะอุโบสถ</p><p>Inside the area, there are important elements to observe, namely the ordination hall, the brick wall around the temple, the stupa containing the relics of Phra Khru Udom Panya, and the Takua Pa Community Museum. Academic work indicates that the museum stores utensils and equipment from the mining era, allowing a visit to the temple to connect with the labor history, trade, and daily life of the town.</p><p>However, no official announcements have been found in 2026 (2569 BE) that confirm the name of the current caretaker, opening hours, areas open for visits, the condition of restoration results after the 2023 (2566 BE) ceremony, or regular temple activities. Therefore, the visiting status should be considered "must check before traveling" and it should not be concluded that the museum or ordination hall is open daily based solely on online information.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=76z9YMImUUk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tesaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality) · Introduction video and 2023 (2566 BE) activity announcements</a> · <a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">วารสารปาริชาต มหาวิทยาลัยทักษิณ (Parichart Journal, Thaksin University)</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ONEP · Cultural environments for conservation</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1847 (2390 BE)</p><h3>Construction of Wat Mai Kamphaeng</h3><p>Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) built the temple after organizing the town wall and the Takua Pa office, constructing a wall and ubosot as the center for town religious ceremonies.</p><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1865 (2408 BE)</p><h3>Phra Khru Udom Panya (Nuam) started as abbot</h3><p>Encyclopedia information states his tenure from 1865 to 1909 and names him the first ecclesiastical provincial governor of Takua Pa.</p><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">8 January 1898 (2440 BE)</p><h3>Received Wisungkhammasima</h3><p>Temple registry sources state the date of receiving Wisungkhammasima as January 8, 1898.</p><a href="https://books.classstart.org/thai-buddhist-temples/03/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1932 (2475 BE)</p><h3>End of the water oath ceremony role in the old regime</h3><p>Temple history data states that the temple was used for the oath of allegiance ceremony until before the 1932 regime change.</p><a href="https://books.classstart.org/thai-buddhist-temples/03/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">1963 (2506 BE)</p><h3>Construction of the current sermon hall</h3><p>The temple database indicates that the reinforced concrete sermon hall was built in 1963.</p><a href="https://books.classstart.org/thai-buddhist-temples/03/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1996 (2539 BE)</p><h3>Registered as a historical site</h3><p>ONEP states that the temple was registered by the Fine Arts Department in 1996, emphasizing the value of the ubosot and Chinese architectural influences.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">2014 (2557 BE)</p><h3>Construction of the chedi for the ashes of Phra Khru Udom Panya (Nuam)</h3><p>Disciples built a chedi to the northeast of the ubosot to contain the ashes of Phra Khru Udom Panya (Nuam).</p><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">26 July 2023 (2566 BE)</p><h3>Ubosot restoration permission ceremony</h3><p>The Takua Pa Town Municipality published news about the Buddhist Lent Day activity and the blessing ceremony to request permission to restore the ubosot, providing evidence of a restoration project during that time, but the success of the work is not verified.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=76z9YMImUUk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Chinese-influenced ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>See the large ubosot which the ONEP database notes was influenced by Chinese architecture.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Museum and antiquities</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Tourism sources mention a museum displaying antiquities, including a wooden pulpit with the royal monogram of King Rama V, but opening hours should be checked on-site.</p><a href="https://live.phuketindex.com/tat-phangnga-6049.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Traces of a historical site</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Visit the temple grounds registered as a historical site in 1996 CE.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa old town landscape</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The temple is located in Takua Pa subdistrict and is one of the old town's key landmarks.</p><a href="https://tatmediaassets.com/file/1097577" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2023-07-20 · Tesaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</p><h3>Takua Pa Town Municipality tours Wat Senanucharangsan and invites participation in the ubosot restoration permission ceremony</h3><p>A municipality promotional video mentions the old town temple and specifies the blessing ceremony to request permission to restore the ubosot on July 26, 2023, alongside the candle procession activity around town.</p><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=76z9YMImUUk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The name "Wat Mai Kamphaeng" comes from the tall brick walls around the temple, not its original official name.</p><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The ordination hall once had a three-tiered roof covered with wooden shingles before being repaired to a single tier with a corrugated iron roof.</p><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The Takua Pa Community Museum inside the temple collects utensils and equipment reflecting the town's tin-mining era.</p><a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Airport</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li><a href="/en/places/wat-boromthat/">Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet (the town's relic temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-khuha/">Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-pathum/">Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-kongkha/">Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-nikorn/">Wat Nikornwararam</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>The temple was founded in 1847 (2390 BE) by Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch na Nakhon), the governor of Takua Pa. The temple registry lists the founding year as 1847 (2390 BE) and as a Mahanikaya private temple.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>See the large ordination hall which the ONEP database indicates was influenced by Chinese architecture. Tourist sources mention a museum displaying artifacts, including a wooden pulpit with the royal initials Jor Por Ror, but you should verify opening conditions on site. See the temple grounds that were registered as an ancient monument in 1996 (2539 BE).</p></details><details><summary>How the municipality document mentions this place</summary><p>The ordination hall architecture features Chinese elements and reflects Thai and Chinese culture.</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Samnakngan Nayobai Lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat Lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) (ONEP) · Art and Cultural Sites to be Conserved</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Phang Nga Province temple registration report</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.50amfh.org/userfiles/file/News/Thai%20temple%20name%20list/south/Phangnga.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Phuketindex-Phang Nga’s Eight Shrines and Eight Temples</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://live.phuketindex.com/tat-phangnga-6049.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">TAT Media Assets-Wat Sena Nucharangsan</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://tatmediaassets.com/file/1097577" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · Encyclopedia of Thai Culture</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://db.sac.or.th/thailand-cultural-encyclopedia/detail.php?id=24480" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ONEP · Cultural environments for conservation</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4412" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">วารสารปาริชาต มหาวิทยาลัยทักษิณ (Parichart Journal, Thaksin University)</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://so05.tci-thaijo.org/index.php/parichartjournal/article/download/219292/168797/871014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Thetsaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality) · Promotional video for activities and ordination hall restoration</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=76z9YMImUUk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">TAT Media Assets · Image of Wat Senanut Rangsan</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://tatmediaassets.com/file/1097561" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>A verified postcode for the temple location could not be found directly from the sources.</li><li>Verified opening hours could not be found.</li><li>Verified admission fees could not be found.</li><li>Verified official phone numbers, Facebook pages, websites, or LINE accounts could not be found.</li><li>Verified information on parking, restrooms, wheelchair access, and dress codes could not be found.</li><li>Verified distances or routes from Takua Pa town centre, Phuket, Khao Lak, and the airport could not be found.</li><li>Evidence confirming the temple's direct role in the tin-mining era could not be found.</li><li>Temple-specific festivals or recommended visiting periods could not be found.</li><li>Nearby places confirmed to be within walking distance are not verified.</li><li>Evidence confirming the current caretaker or abbot in 2026 CE has not been found.</li><li>Opening hours, admission fees, and official announcements stating the ubosot or museum is open daily have not been found.</li><li>Progress reports on the ubosot restoration following the 2023 CE permission ceremony have not been found.</li><li>Specific evidence of how the 2004 CE tsunami damaged the temple has not been found.</li><li>Direct evidence of the temple being abandoned or changing hands has not been found.</li><li>News from the past five years confirming activities or projects outside of the 2023 CE reports has not been found.</li><li>No news in the past 5 years has been found to confirm other activities or projects aside from the 2023 (B.E. 2566) news/media.</li><li>Image from TAT Media Assets with unknown rights. Permission must be requested before use.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
.\audit\jobs\en-attrs.log:882:D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\rest\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Where to Eat and Rest in Takua Pa · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Restaurants, cafes and snack stops in Takua Pa that the guide author has visited, with real opening hours and map links, plus a printable walking leaflet."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Where to Eat and Rest in Takua Pa"><meta property="og:description" content="Restaurants, cafes and snack stops in Takua Pa that the guide author has visited, with real opening hours and map links, plus a printable walking leaflet."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/rest/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/rest/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Takua Pa food, cafés, and places to stop", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/rest/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/rest/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/rest/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/rest/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/" aria-current="page">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/rest/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/rest/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="intro"><a class="eyebrow" href="/en/">Takua Pa 101 / Old Town Guide</a><h1>Take a break, eat and explore Takuapa</h1><p class="lead">Shops and stops personally visited by the people who made this guide, with opening hours and directions</p></div><p>The whole old town is walkable, but there is little shade and the afternoon sun is strong. Stops along the way make it easier to cover the area.</p><p class="small">The list below is selected from<a href="https://siwaracafe.com/guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Siwara Cafe's Takua Pa food and travel guide</a> The people who made this website · Unlike the list of shops on<a href="/en/eat/">the Food and Souvenirs page</a> Every shop listed here has been visited in person, so the opening hours and coordinates are practical and current · Even so, small shops can change their hours at any time, so call ahead before making a long trip.</p><h2>Restaurants and food</h2><div class="rest-stops"><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=Baan%20Lapp%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-baan-lapp.webp" alt="BAAN LAPP (Homey &amp;amp; Steak, Takua Pa)" width="900" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>BAAN LAPP (Homey &amp; Steak, Takua Pa)</h3><p>A warm, homey restaurant and cafe with a Hidden Gem style, serving steak, pasta, and carefully prepared fusion dishes.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 11:00 - 21:00 (closed Wednesdays) · ~2.2 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">From 17:30 onward, the lights create a pleasant atmosphere. It is suitable for a family dinner.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=Baan%20Lapp%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B0%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B9%E0%B9%81%E0%B8%AD%E0%B9%8B%E0%B8%A7" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-moo-satay-kruaew.webp" alt="Kru Aew&#x27;s Khanom Chin and Pork Satay (Thung Phra Pho)" width="1193" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Kru Aew's Khanom Chin and Pork Satay (Thung Phra Pho)</h3><p>A tucked-away shop in Thung Phra Pho serving tender, juicy charcoal-grilled pork satay marinated with spices, hot off the grill with boldly flavored southern Thai khanom chin and curry.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 12:00 - 18:00 (closed Thursdays) · ~2.4 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">The pork satay is grilled fresh, skewer by skewer. A large set of 20 to 30 skewers is recommended with the khanom chin.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B0%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B9%E0%B9%81%E0%B8%AD%E0%B9%8B%E0%B8%A7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-mee-talad-khwang.webp" alt="Phang Nga Noodles, Khwang Market" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Phang Nga Noodles, Khwang Market</h3><p>Springy, tender handmade egg noodles made with an old Hokkien recipe, tossed with fragrant fried garlic oil and large pieces of crispy pork lard, for a balanced, mellow flavor.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 09:00 - 15:30 (closed Tuesdays) · ~0.6 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Try the "special dry tom yum noodles with crispy pork lard". Toss well and enjoy without adding seasoning.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%AE%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%99" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-pa-da-hokkien.webp" alt="Pa Da Hokkien Noodles (Thanon Si Takua Pa)" width="1200" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Pa Da Hokkien Noodles (Thanon Si Takua Pa)</h3><p>A long-running Hokkien noodle shop on Thanon Si Takua Pa. The springy noodles are stir-fried with a fragrant wok-charred aroma, plenty of seafood, and a runny egg.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 07:00 - 17:00 (daily) · ~0.7 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Try the "Hokkien noodles stir-fried with a runny egg". Break the yolk and mix it into the hot noodles for a rich, savory taste.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%AE%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B5%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%99" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%99%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-bang-deen.webp" alt="Bangdeen Chicken Biryani &amp;amp; Curry Roti (Michelin Guide)" width="900" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Bangdeen Chicken Biryani &amp; Curry Roti (Michelin Guide)</h3><p>A well-known Muslim breakfast restaurant recognized by the Michelin Guide. Fragrant, tender spiced chicken biryani is served with a bold dipping sauce, along with soft roti and chicken massaman curry.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 06:00 - 13:30 (daily) · ~2.8 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">The roti dough is freshly kneaded and fried until crisp yet soft. Try the massaman curry roti with hot pulled tea.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%99%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B3%20%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B5" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-pu-dam.webp" alt="Black Crab Restaurant (Bang Nai Si)" width="1010" height="761" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Black Crab Restaurant (Bang Nai Si)</h3><p>A tucked-away restaurant beside a mangrove canal in Bang Nai Si, serving fresh wild black crab with firm, intensely sweet meat and boldly seasoned local-style dishes.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 10:00 - 21:00 (daily) · ~3.2 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Steamed black crab and crab fried rice are the most frequently ordered dishes. Call ahead to check fresh supplies or go before evening.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%B9%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B3%20%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%8B%E0%B8%9C%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%84%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A2%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-kae-padthai.webp" alt="Kae Pad Thai (Takua Pa Old Market)" width="848" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Kae Pad Thai (Takua Pa Old Market)</h3><p>A popular Pad Thai shop in the old town area. Chewy noodles stir-fried in a rich traditional tamarind sauce, with large, fresh, and bouncy shrimp.</p><p class="pick-meta">Open 16:30 to 21:30 (daily) · ~0.6 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Fresh shrimp Pad Thai wrapped in egg is the specialty. Squeeze fresh lime over it and eat with banana blossom and pennywort. Flavorful without extra seasoning.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%8B%E0%B8%9C%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%84%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A2%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%AD%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%99%20%E0%B8%9A%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B3%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%A1" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-jae-ouan.webp" alt="Jae Uan Talay Sod (Baan Nam Kem)" width="900" height="1200" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Jae Uan Talay Sod (Baan Nam Kem)</h3><p>A renowned seafood restaurant in the Baan Nam Kem area. Fresh ingredients delivered directly from fishing boats daily. Spicy seafood dip made with real lime. Evening seaside atmosphere.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: 10:30 to 21:30 (Daily) · ~11.5 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Great for dinner. Large tiger prawns with firm, sweet meat paired with spicy seafood dip.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B9%8A%E0%B8%AD%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%99%20%E0%B8%9A%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B3%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%A1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article></div><h2>Stops and Attractions</h2><div class="rest-stops"><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%96%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-old-town-street.webp" alt="Thanon Si Takua Pa &amp;amp; Old Building Street Art" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Thanon Si Takua Pa &amp; Old Building Street Art</h3><p>An old shophouse district with arched walkways (Five-Foot Way) featuring street art that tells the stories of the Baba people and the town's tin mining history.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: Open all day (09:00 to 17:00 recommended) · ~0.7 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">You can easily walk through various alleys connecting to Tao Sor pastry shops and old town eateries.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%96%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-tam-nang.webp" alt="Tam Nang Waterfall (Si Phang Nga National Park)" width="1200" height="795" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Tam Nang Waterfall (Si Phang Nga National Park)</h3><p>A large waterfall in the middle of a lush rainforest in Si Phang-nga National Park. The water cascades down a 63-meter high cliff into a clear pool where Mahseer barbs swim.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: 08:30 to 16:30 (Daily) · ~21.8 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Between 10:00 and 14:00, the sunlight hits the water surface beautifully. There is a shaded resting spot under large trees.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B3%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%81%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-iron-bridge.webp" alt="Boonsung Iron Bridge (Khok Khanun Bridge)" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Boonsung Iron Bridge (Khok Khanun Bridge)</h3><p>A historical landmark from the prosperous tin mining era. Built from the steel parts of an old tin dredger, elegantly spanning the Takua Pa River.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: Open 24 hours (16:30 to 18:30 recommended) · ~2.3 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Between 17:00 and 18:30, the sunset light shines through the steel bridge structure, turning it into a beautiful orange. Great for taking photos.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B9%87%E0%B8%81%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%8D%E0%B9%88%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-lad-yai.webp" alt="Lad Yai, the old town cultural walking street" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Lad Yai, the old town cultural walking street</h3><p>A classic historical walking street. Thanon Si Takua Pa is closed to traffic so locals can set up stalls selling traditional food, local snacks, and crafts under Sino-Portuguese style lighting.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: Sundays only 15:30 to 20:30 · ~0.7 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">Open only on Sundays. Arriving around 17:00 is recommended when the orange sunlight beautifully lights up the classic old buildings for photos.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%94%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%8D%E0%B9%88%20%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B1%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%9B%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%B2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article></div><h2>Souvenirs</h2><div class="rest-stops"><article class="rest-stop pick"><a class="pick-art" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%8C" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/guide/guide-tuangrat-taosor.webp" alt="Tao Sor Tuang Rat (100-year-old original recipe)" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy"></a><div class="pick-body"><h3>Tao Sor Tuang Rat (100-year-old original recipe)</h3><p>A top souvenir of Takua Pa. Thin crispy crust with dense mung bean filling, scented with traditional candle smoke. The recipe has been passed down through generations.</p><p class="pick-meta">Hours: 08:00 to 17:00 (Daily) · ~0.6 km from Siwara</p><p class="pick-tip">The salted egg yolk filling is their signature. Best bought fresh out of the oven. Can be kept for 7 to 10 days.</p><a class="shop-nav" href="https://maps.google.com/?q=%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%99%E0%B9%8C" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate ↗</a></div></article></div><h2>Walking Pamphlet</h2><div class="leaflet-block"><a class="leaflet-art" href="/assets/leaflet/takuapa-walk-leaflet.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/assets/leaflet/leaflet-page-1.webp" alt="แผ่นพับเดินเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า หน้าแรก" width="1400" height="990" loading="lazy"></a><div class="leaflet-copy"><p>A two-page pamphlet detailing the wooden house and four stops on the route above. Print on A4 paper, fold in half, and carry it with you. No need to look at your phone in the sun.</p><div class="leaflet-actions"><a class="button" href="/assets/leaflet/takuapa-walk-leaflet.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download brochure PDF · 7.6 MB</a><a class="text-link" href="/en/leaflet/">Read on the website instead ↗</a></div><p class="small">Created by<a href="https://siwaracafe.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Siwara Cafe</a> Creators of this guide · Free to distribute and print</p></div></div>
.\audit\jobs\en-attrs.log:883:D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\routes\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Walking Routes Through Takua Pa Old Town · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Four walking routes through Takuapa old town, from temples and pagodas to Chinese shrines, shophouses and the riverside market. Pick one to match the time you have."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Walking Routes Through Takua Pa Old Town"><meta property="og:description" content="Four walking routes through Takuapa old town, from temples and pagodas to Chinese shrines, shophouses and the riverside market. Pick one to match the time you have."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/routes/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/routes/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Old Takua Pa Walking Route", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/r