Tin Mining Era Architecture", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/architecture/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/architecture/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/stories/architecture/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Old houses and architecture</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa stories · Old houses and architecture</span><h1>Buildings built with tin money and dredger steel · Architecture that remembers the town</h1><p class="lead">Read Takua Pa through the shophouses on Culture Street, Boon Sung Iron Bridge, the governor's residence, and Tao Ming School · The transition embedded in brick, mortar, and steel structures</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/tao-ming-school.jpg" width="1477" height="1108" alt="Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you walk through the Thanon Udom Thara and Thanon Si Takua Pa areas today, the first sight is rows of Sino-Portuguese shophouses. The front walkway has a roof cover known as &#x27;Ngo Ka Ki&#x27; which provides shade and rain protection for pedestrians. It feels like stepping back in time to a well-preserved past.</p><p>But these buildings are not just beautiful backdrops for photography. They are a massive archive recording the changes of Takua Pa in the form of brick, mortar, and steel structures. Each piece of architecture tells the story of a town that grew and prospered through tin mining, water trade, and the blending of people from diverse cultures.</p><p>To deeply understand Takua Pa's architecture, we must look beyond its external beauty. We need to distinguish between what is &#x27;newly built&#x27; and what is &#x27;repaired&#x27;, and understand the layers of history hidden within the governor's residence walls, the miners' iron bridge, and the school built from merchants' donations.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The governor's residence and the wall that tells of conflict</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>Back in the era when Takua Pa was growing from the tin mining economy and coastal trade, wealth came with conflicts of interest. The wall of the Takua Pa governor's residence was built to act like a camp wall, protecting against danger and serving as a refuge for townspeople. Scholars suggest, though not settled, that this wall was built during the time when Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) was the governor between 1840 (2383 BE) and 1881.</p><p>The wall was constructed with plaster mixed with gravel and sand, layered with a base wider than the wall itself for stability. The part that remains today is about 5 meters high. The inner area was once divided into a front palace, a main hall, and a rear palace, reflecting the systematic space allocation of the town's rulers in that era.</p><p>Important evidence confirming the prosperity of trade in that era comes from the archaeological excavation by the Fine Arts Department in 1999, which found Qing dynasty Chinese ceramics, European ceramics, glassware, and building decorative parts. These items confirm that this governor's residence was once connected to a global trade network.</p><p>When the city center moved to Ko Kho Khao and Ban Yan Yao, the former governor's residence lost its administrative role. However, today this area has become an open archaeological site that the community uses for cultural events, such as the &#x27;From Takola to Takua Pa&#x27; festival, bringing the old walls back to life as the city's stage.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The year the wall construction began</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Architecture is not just a beautiful backdrop. It is an archive recording the changes of Takua Pa in the form of brick, mortar, and steel structures.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">The district chief's house in the transition era</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/governor-wall.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Governor’s Residence Wall" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Governor’s Residence Wall</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The transition from the governor system to the district system was recorded in the architecture of &#x27;Ban Khun In&#x27;. According to local evidence, this house was built around 1917 (B.E. 2460) by Chinese craftsmen to be the residence of Lieutenant Khun Intharakhiri (Choi na Nakhon), a descendant of the governor lineage who later became the Takua Pa district chief.</p><p>The building is a two-story brick and masonry structure in an applied Sino-Portuguese style, reflecting the architectural influence from port cities like Penang and Singapore that spread through maritime trade. This house therefore not only displays the status of its owner but also serves as evidence showing Takua Pa's connection to the outside world.</p><p>In reading history from architecture, we must be careful to distinguish between the year of construction and the year of repair. A report from the Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning indicates that the Na Nakhon family repaired this house in 1981, trying to preserve the original style but changing the roofing material to corrugated tiles.</p><p>Knowing this repair information helps us see Ban Khun In not just as a static past, but as a living structure that has been maintained and improved by its residents over many decades.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The year Ban Khun In was built (1917)</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>From an industrial structure, the Boon Sung Iron Bridge has changed its role to a community thoroughfare and has become a major landmark attracting people to visit the traces of the mining town.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Educational funds from mining and merchant networks</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/iron-bridge.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Boon Soong Iron Bridge" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The wealth from tin mining was not only spent on building private residences, but also led to the creation of community institutions. The &#x27;Tao Ming School&#x27; building, constructed in 1922 (B.E. 2465), is a clear example of community collaboration.</p><p>Funds for constructing this school came from fundraising among wealthy miners and Chinese merchants from the Old Town market, Yan Yao, Ranong, and Phuket. This shows the strong geographical and economic network of the Chinese community in this region.</p><p>The school building was built by Chinese craftsmen in an applied Sino-Portuguese style and operated as a primary school for a long time. It served as a center for passing on knowledge, language, and culture to generations of Thai-Chinese children in Takua Pa.</p><p>Even though the school has ceased its teaching operations today, the building is still maintained by the Takua Pa Tao Ming School Foundation, standing as a memorial to the community's commitment to education during the town's golden era.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">From a tin dredger to a workers' bridge</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/khun-in.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Khun In House" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Khun In House</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>While residences and schools reflect the wealth of merchants, the &#x27;Saphan Lek Bun Sung&#x27; (Bun Sung Iron Bridge) tells the story of the industrial era and the working class. This bridge was built in 1968 (B.E. 2511) and is a direct product of the city's tin mining industry.</p><p>Before the bridge existed, miners and villagers had to rely on rowboats to cross the Takua Pa Canal, which is over 40 meters wide, making it extremely difficult during the flood season. Although a wooden bridge for pedestrians and bicycles had been built, as the industry expanded, the demand for a stronger structure increased.</p><p>The Juti Boon Sung Dredging Company initiated the construction of this bridge by assembling the steel frames from a decommissioned dredger to form the Khok Khanun iron bridge, which is over 200 meters long. This bridge became an important shortcut connecting the Old Town market area to the mining area, facilitating labor and transportation.</p><p>Although the sound of mining machinery has long gone silent today, the iron bridge remains. It has changed its role from an industrial structure to a community thoroughfare and has become a major landmark that attracts people to visit the traces of the Takua Pa mining town.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Culture Street and shophouses brought back to life</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/shophouse-78.webp" width="400" height="533" alt="Shophouse number 78 on the old town culture street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Shophouse number 78 on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>The shophouses along both sides of Udom Tara and Sri Takua Pa streets are the town's most prominent architectural heritage. These buildings were built to align with the commercial lifestyle, featuring ground-floor spaces for business and connected Ngo Ka Ki walkways. These buildings were influenced by Straits colonial architecture, reflecting the trade networks with Penang and Singapore.</p><p>After the tin mining industry declined and the town's commercial center moved away, this Old Town market area faced quietness. Many buildings were left to decay over time, as if the golden era of this street had passed.</p><p>However, the surviving architecture has become an important asset in revitalizing this area. Around 2007 (B.E. 2550), the &#x27;Takua Pa Old Town Reminiscing&#x27; project was initiated, piloting the opening of the street space as a cultural walking street before developing into the current Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street.</p><p>Every Sunday, this street is closed to traffic and opens as a bustling market. The old Sino-Portuguese buildings return to their role as a backdrop for community activities, including the selling of local food, crafts, and performances, proving that past architecture can find new life and roles in the present day.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-disputed">The year the walking street project started</span></div></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Governor’s Residence Wall</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="governor-wall">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="iron-bridge">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Khun In House</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="khun-in">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Tao Ming School</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="tao-ming">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><aside class="story-outro"><p>Most of the buildings in this article can only be viewed from the outside · One teak house in the old market district has been turned into a cafe, making it one of the few buildings where you can actually go inside to see the wooden structure, ceiling, and interior openings for natural light</p><a class="text-link" href="https://siwaracafe.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The teak house you can enter ↗</a></aside><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The exact years of construction for Ban Khun In and the Takua Pa governor's residence wall</li><li>Documentary information on the later structural repair and restoration of the Boon Sung Iron Bridge</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Old town boundary demarcation project · Takua Pa Old Town</cite> · Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning (ONEP) · PDF pages 55 to 58 · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Tao Ming School building</cite> · สผ. (ONEP) Cultural Art Environment Database · Significance/Characteristics section · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4420" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Khun In building</cite> · สผ. (ONEP) Cultural Art Environment Database · Significance/Characteristics section · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4423" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Governor’s Residence Wall</cite> · Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre (public organisation) · Archaeological highlights · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Thanon Watthanatham Mueang Kao Takua Pa</cite> · Department of Internal Trade · Market records · <span class="source-offline">View sources <small>Original source offline · checked 8 Sep 2026</small></span> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">The year Ban Khun In was built</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Local information states it was built in 1917, but the ONEP report and official databases do not specify a clear construction year. Therefore, primary evidence is needed for confirmation. <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4423" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The year Culture Street started</div><div class="ev-status disputed">DISPUTED</div><div class="ev-explain">The Department of Internal Trade's market registry states the founding or promotion date as 17 December 2017, but news sources and research indicate activities began since 2007. It may therefore be different milestones between event organization and registration. <span class="source-offline">References ↗ <small>Original source offline · checked 8 Sep 2026</small></span></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The year the governor's residence wall was built</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Scholars suggest, though not settled, that it was built during the time of Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch) between 1840 and 1881, but no evidence specifying the exact year construction began has yet been found. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The governor's residence and the wall that tells of conflict</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">The district chief's house in the transition era</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Educational funds from mining and merchant networks</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">From a tin dredger to a workers' bridge</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Culture Street and shophouses brought back to life</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
.\audit\jobs\en-attrs.log:886:D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\stories\city\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>History of Takua Pa: The Town That Moved · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="History of Takua Pa: The Town That Moved"><meta property="og:description" content="From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/city/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "The History of Takua Pa, a Town That Moved with the River", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/city/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/city/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/stories/city/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Know the city</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · About the Town</span><h1>Takua Pa: The Town That Moved with the River</h1><p class="lead">From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered.</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2 is-illustration"><img src="/assets/riverwalk.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takuapa Riverwalk" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Takuapa Riverwalk</span><span class="credit">Illustration · No licensed photo available for this subject yet</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you have a chance to take a walk in Takua Pa old town on a holiday afternoon, you will see old houses, long streets, and a quiet, charming atmosphere. But deep beneath the cracks in the old walls and the slow-flowing river, this town hides a story of major transformation.</p><p>Takua Pa was not located here from the beginning, and it did not always look like this. The town is a living entity that grew, expanded, and moved in rhythm with its resources and the river. From a stopover point on the trans-peninsular route, it became a bustling major market town, and later a town that had to find a new home when the river was no longer favorable.</p><p>We will take you to read Takua Pa through what remains: the governor's residence wall that tells a story of wealth, the old temple by the canal, and the river that was once its main artery. This is a town with more than one version of history.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The Ongoing Debate Over the Town's Name</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/wat-kongkha.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>When speaking of Takua Pa, the first question many people wonder is where the town's name came from. Official sources and historians still offer different explanations, and to date, there is no evidence that can bring this to a single, settled conclusion.</p><p>The first explanation links the town's name to the word "Takola", which appears in ancient records as a major port on the trans-peninsular route. This word is interpreted as a possible root that gradually changed in pronunciation to the current name over several centuries.</p><p>Another explanation is based on local geography and resources. It suggests that the name comes from combining the word "Takua" (which means tin, a mineral abundantly found in the area) and "Pa" (which means forest, reflecting the rich natural environment surrounding the town).</p><p>Although documents from the Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning (ONEP) list both explanations as basic information, they clearly emphasize that there is no definitive evidence to confirm either one. The town's name remains the first mystery greeting visitors, leaving us to imagine the past through different explanations.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The origin of the town's name is not settled</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Takua Pa was not located here from the beginning, and it did not always look like this. The town is a living entity that grew, expanded, and moved in rhythm with its resources and the river.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">The Governor's Residence Wall and Town Defense</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/governor-wall.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Governor’s Residence Wall" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Governor’s Residence Wall</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The image of Takua Pa's past wealth is inextricably linked to the tin mining business. One of the structures that most clearly reflects the power and context of that era is the Takua Pa governor's residence wall, which stands as a historical witness to this day.</p><p>Scholars have suggested, not settled, that this wall was built during the time of Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) while he was the governor between 1840 (2383 BE) and 1881. The wall surrounding the residence was designed as a defensive camp. During an era when the town faced conflicts among Chinese groups linked to the massive benefits from tin mining, the wall served as both a home for the ruler and a fortress to maintain peace.</p><p>The wall's structure is highly stable, constructed with layers of mortar mixed with gravel and sand. The base was designed to be wider than the wall itself. The parts that remain intact today reach a height of about 5 meters. Inside the wall, the area was divided into the front palace, the main hall, and the rear palace, with connecting doors well-proportioned throughout.</p><p>The significance of this governor's residence also appears in historical records when King Chulalongkorn (Rama V) came to inspect the residence on May 28, 1890 (109 RS or 2433 BE). During the excavation by the Fine Arts Department in 1999, Chinese ceramics from the Qing Dynasty and European ceramics were found, providing evidence of Takua Pa's international trade connections.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">The construction year is suggested, not settled, based on the governor's tenure</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">A Town That Grew with the River</h2><p>The Takua Pa River is not just a backdrop for the town, but the main artery that has nourished life and trade since the pioneering era. Official documents explain that Takua Pa old town is an ancient settlement located by the river, and it prospered as a trade center on the trans-peninsular route.</p><p>The bustling water trade and tin mining led to the expansion of an area called Talat Yai (the big market) during the reign of King Rama III, which became an important economic center. Near the hustle and bustle of trade, Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the town's temple) or Wat Kongkha, is situated on the west bank of the Takua Pa canal, serving as the spiritual center for the townspeople.</p><p>The history of Wat Kongkha reflects the complexity of the old town's information. The cultural environment data states that this temple was built in 1762, while the archaeological database uses 1936 as the construction year for the beautiful southern style ordination hall. This hall is decorated with stucco floral patterns and Buddha images, which are still preserved today.</p><p>The differences in these sets of numbers are something we often find in local history. Explaining the town's antiquity through official documents only provides an overview of the settlement in the entire area. It does not mean that every building, every bridge, or every location in the present town has the same age dating back to the town's founding era.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">The age of the location and the age of the building might not be the same year</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">The Day the River Grew Shallow and the Town Had to Move</h2><p>But nature never stays still, and the river is always ready to change. As time passed, the river that built the town began to change its role. ONEP reports note the end of the golden age of Talat Yai, which was primarily caused by geography and changes in resources.</p><p>Long and continuous tin mining caused a massive amount of sand and sediment to flow down and accumulate in the river. The Takua Pa River, which used to be deep enough for cargo ships and passenger boats to navigate easily, gradually became shallower until large-scale navigation declined and became difficult.</p><p>Eventually, when the river was no longer favorable as a center for trade and transportation, moving the town's administrative center was inevitable. Official reports state that Takua Pa had to move its town center to an area called Yan Yao in 1913.</p><p>That relocation marked the end of Talat Yai's prosperity as a center, turning this area into an old town stopped in time with the past. It left behind Sino-Portuguese architecture and traces of development for future generations to experience and study.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">Reasons for the town relocation are based on ONEP reports</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Reading the Town Today</h2><p>Today, Takua Pa old town has come back to life in a different form from the past. The waterfront by the canal, which used to be a port for transporting tin, has now been revived into the area of the Takua Pa riverside market. It becomes lively again from Saturday afternoon to evening, filled with smiles, the aroma of local food, and the chatter of locals.</p><p>The governor's residence wall, once strong, is no longer used to defend against enemies. Instead, it has become a public space open to visitors and is often used as a venue for traditional festivals or major town events. Meanwhile, Wat Kongkha Phimuk continues to function as an active temple with residing monks. It is a peaceful place for meditation and a historical site that is still breathing.</p><p>If you have the chance to walk along the cultural street and the river, what surrounds you are not just stagnant ruins of the past, but evidence of adaptation for survival. The town that moved with the river back then continues to flow and change along with the people today.</p><p>Before fully believing any numbers or legends of the town, we would like to invite you to read and observe this town carefully. The history of Takua Pa is a charming large book. Some pages might be missing due to time, and some pages are still being written by all of us.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takuapa Riverwalk</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="wat-kongkha">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Governor’s Residence Wall</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="governor-wall">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The exact construction year of the governor's residence wall</li><li>Definitive evidence confirming the origin of Takua Pa's name</li><li>The reasons and builders of Wat Kongkha in its early days</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town</cite> · สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Takua Pa Old Town webpage · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town Documents</cite> · สผ. (ONEP) · PDF pages 11-13, 55-56, 58 · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Archaeological Site Database: Takua Pa Town</cite> · ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · List of archaeological sites and suggested city walls · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">The town's name originated from 'Takola' or 'Takua and Pa'</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">ONEP documents list both explanations, but emphasize that there is no definitive evidence to confirm either one. <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The governor's residence wall was built between 1840 and 1881</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">This is suggested, not settled, based on the tenure of Governor Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon), and is not an exact year of construction. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">Wat Kongkha Phimuk was built in 1762</div><div class="ev-status disputed">DISPUTED</div><div class="ev-explain">The cultural environment data states 1762, while the archaeological database uses 1936 as the construction year for the ordination hall. The age of the location and the remaining building might not align. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The Ongoing Debate Over the Town's Name</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">The Governor's Residence Wall and Town Defense</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">A Town That Grew with the River</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">The Day the River Grew Shallow and the Town Had to Move</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Reading the Town Today</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
.\audit\jobs\en-attrs.log:887:D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\stories\food-people\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Peranakan Food in Takua Pa Old Town · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Food serves as both household memories and evidence of cultural exchange · Read Takua Pa through the Baba cuisine, double-layered Tao Sor, and the Culture Str"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Peranakan Food in Takua Pa Old Town"><meta property="og:description" content="Food serves as both household memories and evidence of cultural exchange · Read Takua Pa through the Baba cuisine, double-layered Tao Sor, and the Culture Str"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/food-people/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Takua Pa Baba Cuisine, Flavours of the Old Market Community", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/food-people/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/food-people/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/stories/food-people/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>People and flavors</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · People and Flavors</span><h1>Flavours of the Baba Community and the Old Market</h1><p class="lead">Food serves as both household memories and evidence of cultural exchange · Read Takua Pa through the Baba cuisine, double-layered Tao Sor, and the Culture Street</p><p class="story-meta">Updated 2026-09-07 · 7 min read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2 is-illustration"><img src="/assets/food-center.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takua Pa Open-Air Food Center" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Open-Air Food Center</span><span class="credit">Illustration · No licensed photo available for this subject yet</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>A town that was once a tin mining hub and a port on the trans-peninsular route like Takua Pa has left behind more than just traces of Sino-Portuguese buildings or the operating hours of local markets. It also hides its own stories in the flavours and the way of life of its people.</p><p>In official documents, Takua Pa appears as a community that grew from integrating external cultures. Each dish acts as a reflection of settlement migration, cross-cultural marriages, and the changes in commercial districts through different eras. The flavours of today are edible memories.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">Takua Pa on the Baba Food Map</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>When discussing the food culture on the Andaman coast, the Department of Cultural Promotion officially listed Takua Pa as part of the Baba-Peranakan food communities since its registration as a national cultural heritage in 2013 (2556 BE). This inclusion positions Takua Pa within the network of overseas Chinese descendants who arrived to mine tin and settled down with the locals.</p><p>This knowledge of flavours and cooking methods did not originate from any single isolated group. Instead, it emerged from passing down memories and culinary techniques within families. It is about preserving the identity and taste from generation to generation, whether it is a savoury dish in a regular meal or sweets for important family rituals.</p><p>However, placing Takua Pa on this food culture map serves to explain the overall cultural context. It does not mean that every household or shop in Takua Pa shares the exact same recipe for Moo Hong (braised pork belly), O-tao (fried oyster with taro), or Ang Ku (red tortoise cake). The diversity within each household remains the heart of the local food's vitality.</p></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Reading food menus or observing the local ingredients used in the market is a natural and gradual way of getting to know the way of life and flavours of Takua Pa.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">Double-layered Tao Sor and the Town's Transformation</h2><p>Among the frequently mentioned local foods, Tao Sor (flaky pastry) is a name that clearly appears in government records. The Phang Nga Provincial Tourism and Sports Office notes that Tao Sor is both a famous souvenir and an OTOP (One Tambon One Product) item of Takua Pa, describing it as similar to Chinese mooncakes but smaller, and often topped with sesame seeds.</p><p>The enduring presence of Tao Sor in Takua Pa was also recorded by the Food and Drug Administration during a field inspection in August 2025. It was documented that Tuang Rat shop continues the tradition of making Tao Sor using the traditional double-layered dough, an effort that earned them the FDA Quality Award for outstanding health products in the grassroots economy promotion category.</p><p>The story of Tao Sor helps us see how local sweets can develop and grow alongside the town's economy. At the same time, we cannot jump to the conclusion that Tao Sor originated in Takua Pa as the first in the world, or that there is only one monopolised original recipe here. This is because there is no historical evidence that clearly confirms its exact origin.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Origin and original recipe are not settled</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Culture Street and the Old Commercial District</h2><p>Stepping onto Si Takua Pa Road and Udom Thara Road, we are entering the former commercial centre of the tin mining era. Traces of this golden age are still visible through the architecture of shophouses with Straits colonial influences, featuring covered walkways at the front. Today, these streets have been repurposed into the Takua Pa Old Town Culture Street to draw people back to experience life in the old district.</p><p>The Department of Internal Trade's registry notes that this market is a gathering place for traditional sweets, local food, handicrafts, wickerwork, and cultural activities. The once-sleeping old streets are awakened every Sunday afternoon, creating a space where the younger generation and the original locals can meet and converse through shops and food.</p><p>However, the starting point of these activities still has information that is not entirely agreed upon. Some local news sources state that the retrospective event started in 2007, while official market registries list its founding and promotion in 2017. This discrepancy in information could be interpreted as referring to different milestones, between the community-initiated event and the official registration as a state-promoted market.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-disputed">Starting year of the event has conflicting sources</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Lan Long Market, A Meeting Point of Diversity</h2><p>In addition to the walking street that opens once a week, Takua Pa also has a food hub ready to accommodate people every day, such as the Takua Pa Lan Long Food Centre. This place gathers various main dishes, ranging from rice and curry, made-to-order meals, Khanom Chin (fermented rice noodles), and noodles, to a complete selection of desserts and beverages.</p><p>These places are not just venues for buying and selling goods, but also serve as spaces for the townspeople to exchange conversations. Reading handwritten food menus or observing the local ingredients used in the market is a natural and gradual way of getting to know the way of life and flavours of Takua Pa.</p><p>As for information regarding the food centre's operating hours or the status of specific shops, data on the internet can be inaccurate and is not always updated in real-time. Asking locals on foot or checking directly with the vendors before your trip is a way to ensure you do not miss the chance to taste your favourite dishes.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Operating hours and prices may change according to the season</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Siwara Cafe in the Town's Storytelling System</h2><p>If you visit Takua Pa today, you might see many coffee shops and cafes attempting to present the town's story through their drinks and decor. Siwara Cafe is one of them, and is also the creator of this Takua Pa 101 information. The website has specifically categorised the cafe under the beverage shop section.</p><p>This categorisation and explanation are done so that readers can clearly, transparently, and without confusion separate the role of the information creators from historical facts. As for operating hours, new menus, or prices, readers can check the latest details directly through the shop's communication channels.</p><p>The growth of coffee shops and sitting areas today is a reflection of building upon the drinking and eating culture of the past. It blends with the lifestyles of the younger generation and locals who participate in revitalising the old district, bringing it back to life.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takua Pa Open-Air Food Center</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="food-center">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><aside class="story-outro"><p>This article tells the story behind the flavours. For a list of places that are still open, with opening hours and locations, see the food and travel guide, which the people behind this website visited and tasted for themselves</p><a class="text-link" href="https://siwaracafe.com/guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the Takua Pa food and travel guide ↗</a></aside><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The exact person who invented the first Tao Sor recipe in Takua Pa cannot be confirmed by printed documents.</li><li>The exact and most updated operating hours of markets and shops today.</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>National Intangible Cultural Heritage: Baba Food</cite> · กรมส่งเสริมวัฒนธรรม (Department of Cultural Promotion) · Page 124 (print) / PDF page 133 · <a href="https://book.culture.go.th/newbook/ich/ich2013.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Field news on Phang Nga Tao Sor pastry products</cite> · สำนักงานคณะกรรมการอาหารและยา (Food and Drug Administration) · 2025 (2568 BE) field news · <a href="https://dis.fda.moph.go.th/detail-newsUpdate?id=3271" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Phang Nga Local Products</cite> · สำนักงานการท่องเที่ยวและกีฬาจังหวัดพังงา (Phang Nga Provincial Tourism and Sports Office) · Takua Pa Tao Sor pastry · <a href="https://phangnga.mots.go.th/news/369" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Thanon Watthanatham Mueang Kao Takua Pa</cite> · กรมการค้าภายใน (Department of Internal Trade) · History · <span class="source-offline">View sources <small>Original source offline · checked 8 Sep 2026</small></span> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Experience the Cultural Street</cite> · ผู้จัดการออนไลน์ (Manager Online) · News content referencing 2007 (2550 BE) · <a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9650000044214" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Lan Long Market</cite> · Wongnai · General information · <a href="https://www.wongnai.com/attractions/399190Vq-ตลาดลานโล่ง" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Lan Long Food Court</cite> · เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) · Video description Sep 1, 2024 (2567 BE) · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YBj0I_sMc-I" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Tao Sor pastry originated in Takua Pa and has only one recipe</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">There is no historical evidence clearly stating the exact first origin or establishing a monopoly of a single recipe in Takua Pa. <a href="https://dis.fda.moph.go.th/detail-newsUpdate?id=3271" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The Culture Street event started in 2007 or 2017</div><div class="ev-status disputed">DISPUTED</div><div class="ev-explain">Some news sources cite 2007 for the beginning of the community's retrospective event, but the Department of Internal Trade documents state 2017 as a promoted market. <span class="source-offline">References ↗ <small>Original source offline · checked 8 Sep 2026</small></span></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">Takua Pa on the Baba Food Map</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">Double-layered Tao Sor and the Town's Transformation</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Culture Street and the Old Commercial District</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Lan Long Market, A Meeting Point of Diversity</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Siwara Cafe in the Town's Storytelling System</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
.\audit\jobs\en-attrs.log:888:D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\stories\kuapapoh\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Houses Reopened · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Houses Reopened"><meta property="og:description" content="A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Kuapa Poh, an Old Takua Pa House Brought Back to Life", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/kuapapoh/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/stories/kuapapoh/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>City topics</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · Town Topics</span><h1>Kuapapoh: The Day Old Houses Stand Up to Tell Their Stories</h1><p class="lead">A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" width="1076" height="1521" alt="Kuapapoh, the identity of the old town project" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Kuapapoh, the identity of the old town project</span><span class="credit">โครงการกั่วป่าโพ้ (The Kuapapoh project)</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>Imagine strolling along Sri Takua Pa Road, a street lined with old shophouses that have stood the test of time. Many of these buildings have their wooden doors quietly shut, sleeping away alongside the long-gone golden age of tin mining. Underneath that quietness, however, lie countless memories of the town, its people, and its trade, waiting to be told.</p><p>In recent years, a creative space project called 'Kuapapoh Ji Show Mueang' has stepped in to open the doors of these once-abandoned old houses and bring them back to life. They did not just repaint or repair the roofs, but are bringing the stories of Takua Pa back on display for the newer generations to see.</p><p>This movement reflects a town trying to stand up and tell its own story through the perspectives of locals, artists, and community collaboration. However, in reading these changes, we must still distinguish between newly created narratives for tourism and historical facts that still need careful verification.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The Once Sleeping Spaces Return to the Town</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/shophouse-109.webp" width="400" height="533" alt="Shophouse number 109 on the old town culture street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Shophouse number 109 on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>We often remember Takua Pa through its prosperous tin mining era. When that industry declined, many buildings were left behind. One of them is house number 78, formerly a mine owner's residence and a tin mining company office. Today, this once-empty space has been transformed into the 'Kuapa Mueang Museum', exhibiting mining equipment, old maps, and important documents for visitors to experience the traces of the past.</p><p>Not far away on the same route is house number 109, the building of the Kang Tang Yui Kuan or Wui Kun shrine, an association of the Hakka Chinese. The second floor of the building still houses the deity Tham Kung Ya for people to pay their respects. But the ground floor space, which used to be empty and unused, has been turned into the 'Kuapapoh Gallery' to host exhibitions and showcase the wisdom of the Takua Pa old town residents.</p><p>Another significant building that the project has dusted off is building number 305, or 'Tao Ming School' (the town's first Chinese school), which was an educational center during the prosperous mining era. The team came up with the idea to revive this space under the name 'Tao Ming: The Spark of Learning', using it as a venue to exhibit knowledge on architecture, culture, and traditions related to Takua Pa.</p><p>The effort to bring these three spaces back into use is an initiative filled with the project owners' determination to add economic value and create worth for the town. We must collectively understand, however, that this is an explanation and operation at the community project level, not an official declaration of historical site status or historical endorsement from government agencies.</p></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Working with community narratives is about balancing beautiful memories with facts that must be verified.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">Kuapapoh: A Moniker Carried by the River</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/tao-ming-school.jpg" width="1477" height="1108" alt="Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>As soon as you hear the name 'Kuapapoh', the first question that usually follows is where this word comes from. The project's archival information explains that this was the term Hokkien Chinese used in the past to call Takua Pa town, dividing the translation into two parts: 'Kuapa' meaning Takua Pa, and 'Poh' meaning market or port.</p><p>When combined, the word conveys 'Takua Pa port market', reflecting the past image of Chinese people sailing across the sea to trade and mine tin, using the pier as a center for exchanging both goods and culture. In addition, in the community's belief, the word Kuapapoh is also embedded in the rituals performed at various shrines during the vegetarian festival, becoming a word with deep meaning in the hearts of the townspeople.</p><p>While this is a beautiful story that connects well with the community's roots, in academic circles, the origin of the Takua Pa town name is still debated with several suggested, not settled hypotheses. Some refer to the ancient name Takola, while others base it on the words takua (lead) and pa (forest).</p><p>Therefore, the project's explanation is considered a record of memory and interpretation through the context of the Hokkien Chinese community, rather than a single, settled historical academic conclusion. This is the reason we must read this story with an open mind and caution.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">not settled academically</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Peranakan Charm Through the Brush Tip</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/tao-ming.webp" width="1000" height="667" alt="Tao Ming School" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The liveliness of the old town district reappeared concretely in September 2026 (2569 BE) when 78 Studio on Takua Pa Culture Street opened its space to host a solo art exhibition titled 'Peranakan Charm · Still Becoming'.</p><p>The artist behind these works is Tinnaphop Ngansathir, or Chang Pong. He is a native of Takua Pa old market, growing up with cartoon drawings, superheroes, and Chinese deities familiar in the local culture. After accumulating artistic experience for a long time, he chose to use his brush tip to convey the stories of the town he is attached to.</p><p>The artworks in the exhibition focus on extracting Peranakan colors, patterns, and clothing for display, serving as a bridge connecting the glorious past with people in the present era. Each painting is not just visually beautiful, but an emotional record of the artist towards the town.</p><p>Even though the project documents clearly record the exhibition hosting and the opening of space for artists to express themselves, what we still do not see in the reports are the number of attendees or a formal economic impact assessment. These activities thus serve as cultural ripples rather than official government statistics.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Viewing the Town Through the Lens of Six Heritages</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" width="640" height="145" alt="Peranakan tile patterns still visible in the old town" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Peranakan tile patterns still visible in the old town</span><span class="credit">โครงการกั่วป่าโพ้ (The Kuapapoh project)</span></figcaption></figure><p>To help visitors understand the identity of Takua Pa old town more deeply, the project did not stop at organizing exhibitions. They also compiled and presented the '6 Or' concept to use as a framework for viewing the town's heritage hidden in daily life.</p><p>This concept starts with 'Aphon' (Apparel) or local clothing, whether it is lace fabrics, batik sarongs, and various accessories. Followed by 'Asai' (Abode) which covers the way of life, trading, and living in accordance with the times. As for 'Ahan' (Food), it is indispensable. It is the traditional flavors passed down from generation to generation, and the old restaurants that still keep their stoves on to this day.</p><p>In the dimension of the mind, the town is driven by 'Arom' (Emotion) expressed through art, music, and the sound of drums in the vegetarian festival. Meanwhile, 'Aram' (Temples and Shrines) is the center of faith, including temples, shrines, and Yui Kuan scattered around the old town district. And finally, 'Akhan' (Architecture), the structures that stand as witnesses to change.</p><p>All these concepts were also further developed in the design of souvenirs, such as the Poh Shop project's t-shirts. The revenue from the sales is brought back to fund the restoration and drive the town's activities forward, truly circulating value back to the community.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">What the Town Tells and What We Must Verify</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Kuapapoh project allows us to see the determination of the new generation and locals who want to preserve and build upon the stories of Takua Pa. They created creative spaces, brought in art as a medium, and turned once-closed houses into new travel destinations.</p><p>What is recorded on the project's website and documents serves as a contemporary archive that the community chose to tell about itself. It is a memory full of life and the pride of the residents.</p><p>At the same time, however, reading these stories also calls for us to maintain a balance between appreciation and historical facts. Many parts of the information are explanations that have not been endorsed by government agencies and cannot replace calendars or academic documents.</p><p>This is the challenge and fun of walking around Takua Pa old town today. We have the task of listening to those beautiful stories while simultaneously asking questions and finding answers from reliable sources, so that our journey does not end with just looking at pictures, but truly understanding the town in all dimensions.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Tao Ming School</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="tao-ming">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takuapa Riverwalk</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The number of attendees or the official economic impact generated by the project</li><li>The legal status or endorsement from government agencies of the three houses</li><li>The clear and settled origin of the Takua Pa town name in history</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Kuapapoh · Project Archives</cite> · Kuapapoh Project · content.json · origin, events, openings sections · <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Defining houses number 78, 109, and 305 as creative spaces</div><div class="ev-status qualified">QUALIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">This is an explanation from the project owner, not a declaration of historical site or space status from a government agency. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The origin of the word Kuapapoh translates to Takua Pa port market</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The origin of the Takua Pa town name in historical work still has several suggested, not settled hypotheses, thus the project's explanation should not be used as a settled academic conclusion. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The hosting of the Peranakan Charm exhibition by Tinnaphop Ngansathir in September 2026 (2569 BE)</div><div class="ev-status qualified">QUALIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Referenced from project data, but not a retrospective official calendar confirming attendee numbers or economic impact. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The Once Sleeping Spaces Return to the Town</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">Kuapapoh: A Moniker Carried by the River</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Peranakan Charm Through the Brush Tip</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Viewing the Town Through the Lens of Six Heritages</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">What the Town Tells and What We Must Verify</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div><section class="research-section" id="project-gallery"><h2>Images from Kuapapoh Project Archive</h2><div data-gallery><div class="gallery-tabs" hidden><button type="button" data-tab="today">Present photos</button><button type="button" data-tab="archive">Historical photos</button></div><div id="gallery-today" data-panel="today"><h3>Present photos</h3><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · กั่วป่าโพ้ แบรนด์เมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" width="1076" height="1521" alt="Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Town Brand" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Town Brand<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันเมืองเก่า"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" width="640" height="145" alt="Old Town Peranakan Tile Patterns" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Old Town Peranakan Tile Patterns<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure></div></div><div id="gallery-archive" data-panel="archive"><h3>Historical photos</h3><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-history.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · แผ่นเล่าประวัติกั่วป่าโพ้"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-history.jpg" width="1078" height="1519" alt="Kuapapoh History Board" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Kuapapoh History Board<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-wuikun.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · หวุ่ยกุ่น"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-wuikun.jpg" width="1086" height="1448" alt="Wui Kun" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Wui Kun<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-1.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 1"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-1.jpg" width="900" height="1272" alt="Takua Pa Old Document 1" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Takua Pa Old Document 1<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-2.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-2.jpg" width="900" height="1268" alt="Takua Pa Old Document 2" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Takua Pa Old Document 2<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure></div></div></div></section></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
